mahesh kumar was supposed to start  preparing his fields for planting rice  three weeks ago but this is the first  day that the water supply has come  after weeks of power cuts resulting in a  lack of water mahesh doesn't know how  long today's supply will last  we have to till the field add manure use  the tractor and for all this we need  regular water supply from the time it's  sown to its harvest if there is  irregular supply either the quality of  rice will deteriorate or the plant will  die the farmer will suffer if any of  this happens  homes and businesses have also been  suffering power cuts for weeks this  hospital in uttar pradesh has been  without electricity for at least six  hours a day forcing staff to use diesel  generators when the price of the fuel is  at a record high  we are spending more than 150 dollars  every day on diesel we're not passing  the burden onto the patients right now  but if these power outages continue  we'll be forced to increase patients  fees  the cuts are due to a severe shortage of  coal at power stations  experts blame the huge demand for  electricity during a heat wave coupled  with bottlenecks and transportation and  sky rocketing prices of imported coal  with climate change exacerbating heat  waves environmentalists say the answer  to meet rising electricity needs isn't  to open more coal mines as india is  doing because that will increase  greenhouse gases that trap more heat  seventy percent of india's electricity  comes from coal-fired power stations  analysts say the latest crisis is a  reminder to invest more in green energy  when i say renewable energy the whole  ecosystem needs to be developed because  renewable energy is intermittent source  of power again we rely on imported uh  modules and cells for you know building  up solar capacity or when capacity in  india so we need to build across the  value chain  the international atomic energy agency  says demand for electricity in india is  expected to increase more than anywhere  else in the world over the next 20 years  as the country develops  as mahesh uses the scarce water to clean  himself and cool off  he says his livelihood depends on the  supply continuing to flow elizabeth  paranam al jazeera new delhi
 

